    "turn_text": "It's one of the things that is completely hard to understand. And in part you have to mention the kind of the inner-ignum or what happened in between. It was vindicating to see Clinton sign it and then embrace it and claim it as his. But the real test was, well, what would it do? And welfare roles plummeted by any fair analysis for those who could work, they in fact did work. And then interview after interview, they would themselves say, man am I glad somebody got me off the couch and out from in front of the TV and out working. that led to a boom in the economy and more employment and people feeling better about themselves living productive lives and then making vastly more money than they could make on welfare. As you said, in a stunningly short period of time, I mean, if it had changed in 50 years, you might have understood it, but it changed, like you said, in 10 to 15 years, it changed very rapidly. And we kind of woke up one day. There was a Democrat majority that came along and Nancy Pelosi and her crew and suddenly the work requirements were gone and you discover, well, the work requirement in this particular program went away this year and you'd focus on that and then you'd look around and say, wow, it went away in that program and that",
